Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> James wrote:

> > I think the key here is the idea of resources, and that hit
> > points are also a resource.  If you're a Valk, dual
> > weaponning Excalibur and a long sword, with 15 daggers
> > in your quiver, decent armor and something like 150 hit
> > points, it's better to mel=E9e that mumak,

> A valid approach for that case.

> Make that 50 HP's (after all we're talking not only about high end
> characters) and this tactics might quickly turn out to be bad if
> the mumak can place a good hit at you (especially if the mumak is
> not the only monster nearby).

One of the things I've noticed is that the fighter roles (like
Valk and Samurai) tend to get maximum hit points faster; a Valk
with maximum 50 hit points probably won't be meeting mumaks.

Of course, if earlier mumaks have pushed her actual hit point
count down to those levels, avoiding mel=E9e is important.  The
point is, as always, which resource you can most afford to
spare, and if your hit points are that low, then it isn't hit
points.

Also, let's not forget the option of avoiding it until you're
hit points are back up to where you can afford mel=E9eing.  That
way, you keep both the hit points, and your daggers.

> > so that the daggers
> > are still in your quiver if a mind flayer shows up
> > immediately after your fight (before you've had time to pick
> > up the daggers).

> If I had 15 daggers (as in your example) I wouldn't mind throwing
> (if necessary) 6 at the mumak, there are still enough for the MF
> (plus your berserker melee factors that you assumed above still
> available, optionally).

> But here again the time spent is a factor; you don't want both of
> those two monsters to be adjacent to you since both of their melee
> attacks are of the worse sort (large damage, brain sucking). Killing
> or significantly weakening at least one from distance is my approach.

If you know what's coming, there are often many options
available; getting to a bottleneck where you can destroy the
mumak, for example, then back up and use the daggers on the mind
flayer.  IMHO, knowledge and speed are even more important than
good attacks.  My point is just that I like to always have a
ranged attack available, in case something disagreeable pops up
unexpectedly.  And that using ranged attacks temporarily reduces
your possiblities of using them again.  It's a point that I
think has to be weighed in the balance---ignoring ranged attacks
is definitly a bad strategy, but forgetting that they cost
resources isn't going to win many games either.  What makes this
game so interesting is precisely that there isn't any one tactic
that is best in every case.

